WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.340
Terminator movies: some are good, some&nbsp;
are bad, and some are harder to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:10.500
than a nuclear apocalypse. Here's what we love&nbsp;
— and hate — about this iconic film franchise.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:16.140
As maligned as its reputation may be among&nbsp;
fans and critics, Terminator Salvation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.880
a criminally under-appreciated entry in the&nbsp;
wider Terminator franchise. One of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.500
that makes the 2009 film stand apart from the&nbsp;
rest is that it makes an effort to break from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.480
the usual narrative formula of the series, trying&nbsp;
to change what a Terminator movie can be. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.340
the film itself leaves room for improvement,&nbsp;
Salvation offers a surprisingly refreshing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.780
installment in the franchise, especially&nbsp;
compared to its more contrived follow-ups.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.340
"Come with me if you wanna live."

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.740
After several movies teasing an epic war for&nbsp;
survival between humanity and sentient machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.240
Terminator: Salvation is actually set&nbsp;
during the post-apocalyptic conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.220
after Skynet has unleashed a devastating&nbsp;
nuclear attack. Rather than relying on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.720
standard Terminator plotline of protagonists&nbsp;
on the run from a relentless killing machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.400
Salvation is a war story, one that finds&nbsp;
humanity on the back foot. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.920
a prophesized hero embracing their wartime&nbsp;
destiny, Salvation follows a rugged survivor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.480
as he attempts to navigate the war-torn&nbsp;
world — and it's all the better for it.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.100
The most obvious shortcoming of the&nbsp;
Terminator franchise is that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.140
way too many sequels and reboots to the first&nbsp;
two movies. These follow-ups tend to get lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.700
in their own failed attempts to continue the&nbsp;
original story, often playing with continuity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.680
in radically different ways while noticeably&nbsp;
suffering from diminishing returns in quality.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:39.360
Since 1991's Judgment Day, there have&nbsp;
been four additional Terminator movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.080
none of which reached the critical&nbsp;
acclaim of the first two films. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.040
franchise reached its low point&nbsp;
in 2015 with Terminator Genisys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.100
which holds a paltry 26% critics' score on&nbsp;
Rotten Tomatoes. Though 2019's Terminator:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.260
Dark Fate enjoyed the highest acclaim since&nbsp;
Judgment Day, with a 70% Rotten Tomatoes score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:04.080
it was still a financial disappointment, earning&nbsp;
just $261 million at the worldwide box office.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.800
Composer Brad Fiedel's theme and score for the&nbsp;
first two Terminator movies are relatively simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.100
but instantly recognizable. The 1984 original&nbsp;
film features a stripped-down industrial sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:23.100
driven largely by synthesizers, oscillators, and&nbsp;
ambient noise to set the mood. These minimalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.960
sensibilities balance the grounded '80s&nbsp;
setting with the futuristic stakes at play.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:33.600
Fiedel returned to score Judgment Day. For&nbsp;
the sequel, he maintained the iconic themes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.520
from his previous work while upping the ante&nbsp;
in terms of instrumentation. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.160
he introduced full orchestration to the score,&nbsp;
allowing for a fuller and warmer sound in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.420
to take advantage of the heightened scope&nbsp;
and elevate the story's quieter moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.340
Fiedel's work on the score for the first two&nbsp;
movies subtly laid the musical foundations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.020
for the entire franchise, and his influence&nbsp;
has been felt across every subsequent film.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.720
"What day is it? The date!"

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.880
"12th... May... Thursday."

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:03.780
"What year?!"

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.460
The first few Terminator movies take a&nbsp;
relatively simple approach to time travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.980
mechanics –- soldiers from the future travel back&nbsp;
in time to alter the course of history by killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.720
those who would shape humanity's destiny.&nbsp;
Salvation largely shirks the time travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.880
component of the franchise altogether, telling&nbsp;
a complete story set within one relative period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.200
without anyone traveling through the time&nbsp;
stream. This tight approach to time travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.960
descends into progressively more complicated&nbsp;
timeline mechanics after Salvation, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.700
making the science fiction elements more&nbsp;
confusing and intrusive to each film's story.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.700
Genisys introduces the concepts of temporal&nbsp;
paradoxes and divergent timelines caused by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.200
tampering with the space-time continuum, with&nbsp;
the characters' lives affected by these drastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.360
changes. Dark Fate goes one step further&nbsp;
by completely changing the future conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.280
as a different sentient A.I. replaces&nbsp;
Skynet and successfully assassinates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.840
John Connor before he can save humanity,&nbsp;
disrupting the original timeline. Both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.700
these films take a relatively clean take on&nbsp;
the concept of time travel and muddle it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.080
leading to a confusing and entirely&nbsp;
unnecessary mish-mash of continuities.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.960
The first couple of Terminator movies use pacing&nbsp;
in a similar way. With the Terminators scouring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.580
the greater Los Angeles area for their intended&nbsp;
targets, the lead characters are forced on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.380
run before regrouping to take on the antagonist.&nbsp;
This premise provides a perfect opportunity for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.540
filmmakers to ratchet up the tension as the&nbsp;
heroes lie low, only barely evading their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.580
cybernetic pursuers before the big action set&nbsp;
pieces kick off. When the movies do cut loose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.200
for these action sequences, they deliver on&nbsp;
this sudden, cathartic release of tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.600
and suspense to great effect. All in all, the&nbsp;
Terminator movies are a masterclass in action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.220
movie pacing, knowing just when to come&nbsp;
up for air — before diving right back in.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.180
The Terminator franchise has always&nbsp;
enjoyed the occasional moment of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.140
employed throughout the movies to break up&nbsp;
the tension for the audience. What the films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.980
following Judgment Day tend to forget,&nbsp;
however, is that the funnier moments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:09.600
the first two movies were subtly employed so&nbsp;
as to not detract from the narrative stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.640
and overarching tone. The questionable use of&nbsp;
humor is apparent as early as Terminator 3:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.840
Rise of the Machines, and does not&nbsp;
noticeably improve in its follow-ups.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.040
"Talk to the hand."

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.440
Genisys and Dark Fate continue to poke fun at&nbsp;
the characters in increasingly awkward ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.780
with the latter portraying a retired T-800&nbsp;
who became an interior decorator. Though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.860
early movies were not without levity, the&nbsp;
later Terminator entries' sense of humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.780
ventures into camp and self-parody in ways that&nbsp;
threaten to derail each story's atmosphere.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.100
While the franchise as a whole is&nbsp;
known for its bombastic action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:48.840
the original Terminator opts for a more&nbsp;
grounded, intimate approach to its thrills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:54.720
The first movie is almost paced like a slasher&nbsp;
movie, as the T-800 brutally eliminates anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.280
who gets in its way. The only major things&nbsp;
differentiating the Terminator from typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.680
slashers is a preference for guns over knives&nbsp;
— and a metallic endoskeleton, of course.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:10.020
These horrific sensibilities are on full&nbsp;
display when the T-800 breaks into Sarah's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:15.600
apartment and kills her roommate, Ginger, along&nbsp;
with Ginger's boyfriend. Sarah desperately calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.620
her apartment to warn her friends of the&nbsp;
threat… but they're already preoccupied.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.140
In true slasher movie fashion, after their&nbsp;
romantic interlude, the couple are murdered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.640
by their stalker. This moment showcases&nbsp;
just how relentless the Terminator can be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.020
while demonstrating that the movie is&nbsp;
just as terrifying as it is exciting.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.120
"I'll be back."

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.500
Arnold Schwarzenegger's career was&nbsp;
catapulted to new heights by The Terminator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.940
and the franchise has always maintained its&nbsp;
connection to the legendary action hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:50.220
Though Schwarzenegger is a welcome sight in the&nbsp;
first few films, the franchise goes to ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.380
lengths to bring him back and allow him to&nbsp;
reprise his star-making role. By Salvation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.540
it starts to feel like the narrative needs to move&nbsp;
beyond Schwarzenegger, but the film series itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.860
has always existed in the actor's shadow in a&nbsp;
way that prevents it from forging new ground.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.380
Salvation was produced while Schwarzenegger was&nbsp;
serving as Governor of California, prompting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:16.860
2009 movie to use CGI and a body double instead.&nbsp;
The appearance feels forced and distracts from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:21.960
the movie's climax rather than adding any sense&nbsp;
of resonance or relevance to the finale. Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:28.320
Fate also shoe-horns Schwarzenegger's T-800 into&nbsp;
the story, with the cyborg living in self-imposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.120
retirement for decades before emerging to&nbsp;
conveniently team up with Sarah late in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.500
film. It's past time for the Terminator&nbsp;
franchise to move past its biggest star.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:44.520
Judgment Day introduces the fearsome T-1000, a&nbsp;
more advanced Terminator from the post-apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.740
future. The T-1000's unique composition allows&nbsp;
him to transform his body into different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:55.380
forms as he stalks John and Sarah Connor. A&nbsp;
breathtaking technical achievement in 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:01.740
the special effects to bring the T-1000 to life&nbsp;
still hold up today as a visually impressive feat.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:07.380
James Cameron worked closely with Industrial Light&nbsp;
&amp; Magic on Judgment Day, with approximately $5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:12.540
million from the production budget spent on visual&nbsp;
effects. This investment more than paid off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:18.300
with the liquid metal arguably standing out as&nbsp;
the most memorable visual effect in the franchise.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.520
The Terminator films following Judgment&nbsp;
Day each have a tendency to employ twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.540
late in the story to surprise their&nbsp;
audiences and up the stakes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:32.340
these twists rarely connect with audiences and&nbsp;
serve more as distractions than anything else.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:37.800
For example, Terminator: Genisys posits&nbsp;
that an alternate timeline version of John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:43.020
has been transformed into a time-traveling&nbsp;
Terminator himself. Dark Fate, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:49.020
reveals that the retired T-800 has secretly been&nbsp;
feeding Sarah information to track down other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:53.760
Terminators, a plot point that makes less and&nbsp;
less sense the more one thinks about it. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.320
it could always be worse: In the unused&nbsp;
original ending for Terminator Salvation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:04.080
John Connor is killed off altogether, before&nbsp;
his face is grafted onto Marcus' — who then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.620
unceremoniously massacres the rest&nbsp;
of the movie's lead characters.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.320
At a time when female action protagonists were&nbsp;
a rare sight in major Hollywood productions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:18.600
Linda Hamilton's Sarah Connor became one of&nbsp;
the most recognizable sci-fi action heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.971
in history. The Terminator sees Sarah&nbsp;
transform from a normal woman in a dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.971 --> 00:09:28.020
end job to a hardened fighter who delivers&nbsp;
the killing blow against the T-800. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:32.700
character evolution continued into Judgment&nbsp;
Day and Dark Fate, with Sarah becoming a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.480
seasoned veteran who is quite capable&nbsp;
of taking down Terminators by herself.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:37.560
"I'll be back"

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:48.600
Hamilton's performance is a huge part of Sarah&nbsp;
Connor's depiction as not just as a survivor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.660
but as a warrior, too. Hamilton made Sarah a&nbsp;
bonafide action hero, channeling her trauma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:01.020
into a steely resolve. To balance this, Hamilton&nbsp;
displays a tragic vulnerability in Sarah too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:04.200
giving the franchise its most&nbsp;
nuanced performance by far.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:09.840
There are a few effective plot twists in the&nbsp;
Terminator franchise, but some of them were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.940
infamously spoiled for audiences by the movies'&nbsp;
marketing campaigns. In the beginning of Judgment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:20.700
Day, the T-800 is positioned as another model&nbsp;
sent back to assassinate John before it saves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:25.740
him from the T-1000 in the nick of time. It's a&nbsp;
thrilling twist if you don't know it's coming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.900
but this heroic change in direction for the&nbsp;
T-800 was spoiled in the film's trailers.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.100
"This time, there are two. Terminator 2."

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:42.420
Meanwhile, Salvation revolves around&nbsp;
Marcus discovering he's a cyborg after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:46.860
he's inspected by human freedom fighters&nbsp;
during the war against the machines. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.360
twist was similarly spoiled in the trailers&nbsp;
and television ads for the film; as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.120
audiences were aware of Marcus' cybernetic&nbsp;
secret long before he discovered it for himself.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.060
Armed with a whole arsenal of weaponry and some of&nbsp;
the most iconic catchphrases in cinematic history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.140
Arnold Schwarzenegger became a household&nbsp;
name thanks to The Terminator. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.880
1984 film almost totally revolves around&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger's performance as the T-800,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:17.220
with his stoic demeanor and chiseled physique&nbsp;
defining the franchise as a whole. Seven years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.660
after portraying the ultimate sci-fi&nbsp;
antagonist, Schwarzenegger's turn as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:27.120
very different T-800 in Judgment Day effectively&nbsp;
reframed him as the heroic face of the franchise.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.800
There's a reason why the film series keeps&nbsp;
bringing back Schwarzenegger to reprise his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.660
fan-favorite role, even when it works to the&nbsp;
story's detriment. Decades after his debut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:42.120
as the T-800, Schwarzenegger never feels like&nbsp;
he's phoning it in as the futuristic killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.180
even with lesser material&nbsp;
to work with in later films.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:51.600
The Terminator series is prone to the worst kind&nbsp;
of sequels — ones that not only dilute the quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.820
of the franchise, but also actively make plot&nbsp;
points from previous installments irrelevant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:02.100
This unfortunate trend begins as early as Judgment&nbsp;
Day, with the revelation that Terminators have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:07.260
shifted their priorities towards assassinating&nbsp;
John after failing to kill Sarah. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:12.180
the subsequent set of sequels do even further&nbsp;
harm to the overarching story and its legacy.

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:17.400
For example, Rise of the Machines reveals that&nbsp;
machine-driven Armageddon was inevitable all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.380
along, rendering Sarah and John's efforts&nbsp;
in the second movie totally useless. Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.160
Fate is the most egregious of all in&nbsp;
this regard, with the movie opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:30.720
with a T-800 successfully assassinating&nbsp;
John before he can fulfill his destiny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.280
This nullifies the struggles that Sarah&nbsp;
endured to survive in the preceding films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:38.820
and the sequel scrambles to find&nbsp;
new ways to continue the story.

